194879 #edb/ref 1080 "Z" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194879 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194879 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5cc5af4f-fa18-5a12-9d4b-405238a0c985" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198102 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198102 #edb/ref 1071 "Write down $\\lnot P(x)$ given that\n![](images/ed/ed810318e9b5d6e76b77735530ff9312af66f936d7c8e197d6dd0fb99227dcb8.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198102 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12d987fd-f966-522d-9370-48f7306930ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198102 #edb/ref 1074 "The symbol \"$\\neg$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n![](images/97/976e6fe9a107732c81210776e49cfe3f68939512c0fad63739e2f59acb17f109.svg) \n\nIn other words, $x$ is smaller than $10.$ Therefore, $\\neg P(x)$ means the following:\n![](images/dc/dca132f34f73e2f5987a803e592c1f664cc790ed83ec204205975c401280038c.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198103 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198103 #edb/ref 1071 "The shaded region below represents the truth set of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Determine the region that represents the truth set of $\\neg P(x).$ \n\n![](images/cc/cc80d49abe6293acb1325035799dac833a9bcb8f485ed1f785f78c4e1f39b8be.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fcee0d41-9ff9-5f84-b8fe-73bfad36c7bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198103 #edb/ref 1074 "The truth set of $\\neg P(x)$ is the *complement* of the truth set of $P(x){:}$\n\n$$\nT_{\\neg P} = \\overline{T}_P\n$$\n\nTherefore, to get the truth set of $\\neg P(x),$ we take all the points that belong to $U$ and do not lie in $T_p,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86205c3b03c01581677b4fa877dae8c4ca743fe0ac0f1673ef504d548e8c785d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198111 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198111 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\qquad$ $x \\nleq \\sqrt{2}$\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Write down $\\neg P(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198111 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "adacf679-91a6-500f-b13a-18418d851d5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198111 #edb/ref 1074 "In plain English, our predicate $P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x$ is not smaller than or equal to $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nThe symbol \"$\\neg$\" represents negation. To negate our plain English expression, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ It is not true that $x$ is not smaller than or equal to $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nIn other words, $x$ *is* *smaller than or equal to* $\\sqrt{2}.$ Therefore, $\\neg P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x\\leq \\sqrt{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1039 "Negating a Predicate Expressed in Plain English" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198457 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "391bb8ae-88ba-520c-a5cf-7e2fa5474788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198458 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198458 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198458 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198458 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198458 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198458 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198458 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198458 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198458 #edb/ref 1039 "Negating a Predicate Expressed Using Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198458 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198458 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7943a26b-d4fa-5ce1-bc47-5ea4873fc367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198459 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198459 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198459 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198459 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198459 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198459 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198459 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198459 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198459 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198459 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198459 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Truth Set of Predicate's Negation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198459 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198459 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "adbddf14-3aca-50dd-80f8-4146193c91ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198515 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198515 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198515 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198515 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198515 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198515 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198515 #edb/ref 1045 "The \"Not\" Connective With Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198515 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198515 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198515 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198515 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fa35d201-6dec-5ad9-8120-8f14bb512b33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198708 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198708 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198708 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198708 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the truth set of $¬P(x)$ given the following predicate defined over the universal set of positive integers between $1$ and $10,$ inclusive: \n\n$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x$ is prime" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198708 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46ce81ed-7f98-523e-8348-7d75c19c5c70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198708 #edb/ref 1074 "The truth set of $¬P(x)$ is the *complement* of the truth set of $P(x):$\n\n$$\n{T}_{¬P}={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P},\n$$\n\nThe truth set of $P(x)$ is the set of primes between $1$ and $10,$ inclusive, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\{2,3,5,7\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{¬P} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{\\{2,3,5,7\\}} \\\\  & =\\{1,4,6,8,9,10\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199416 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199416 #edb/ref 1061 62556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199416 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "689313ef-ffe1-58c6-8b2b-9b55b7db42a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199416 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199417 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199417 #edb/ref 1061 62557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199417 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f06746b0-83de-5f4b-aa37-7e80db4e4a56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199417 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199418 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199418 #edb/ref 1061 62560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199418 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0d9a565f-fee0-59f8-bc74-1f86aac39bf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199418 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199419 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199419 #edb/ref 1061 62563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199419 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b38cd7f4-1fed-50b1-8e85-1cc61e0ab9f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199419 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199420 #edb/ref 1061 62564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199420 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "36c3a3d4-465b-5645-a63b-4df3e07d3a80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199420 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199522 #edb/ref 1064 "The **negation** of the predicate $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lnot P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\n$$\n\nThis predicate is true if and only if $P$ is false for a specific set of assignment variables $x_1,x_2, \\ldots x_n.$\n\nFor example, consider the following predicate defined over the universal set $\\mathbb Z.$\n\n$\\qquad$ *$P(m):$ $m$ is even*\n\nThen, $\\lnot P(m)$ is a new predicate that reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$m$ is **not** even*\n\nThis can be simplified as:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$m$ is odd*\n\nFor example:\n\n- $P(2)$ is true while $\\lnot P(2)$ is false\n\n- $P(3)$ is false while $\\lnot P(3)$ is true" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199522 #edb/ref 1065 7988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199522 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "282e591f-3d63-58ed-8e4e-852ba57975cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199522 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199543 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $T_P$ be the truth set of the predicate $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n).$\n\nFrom the definition of the negation of a predicate, we have that $\\lnot P$ is true if and only if $P$ is false. Therefore, the truth set of $\\neg P$ is the *complement* of the truth set of $P{:}$\n\n$$\nT_{\\neg P} = \\overline{T}_P\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following predicate defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}{:}$\n\n$P(x):$ $-2x +3 \\leq 5$\n\nTo find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & \\le 5 \\\\ -2x & \\le 2 \\\\ x & \\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = [-1,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{¬P} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{[-1,\\infty )} \\\\  & =(-\\infty ,-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199543 #edb/ref 1065 8141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199543 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3dd43712-0f20-5210-9cba-7ef53f91ef83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199543 #edb/ref 1067 "Truth Sets of Negations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381516 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $5$ divides $x.$\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "159e411c-061f-56e2-a816-7376959fd683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381516 #edb/ref 1074 "The symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $5$ divides $x.$\n\nIn other words, $5$ does *not* divide $x.$ Therefore, $¬P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $5$ does **not** divide $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381517 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381517 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381517 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186381520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381517 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "289a3926-2084-50a5-b7fc-125f3b8026c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381518 #edb/ref 1080 "$5$ divides $x$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381518 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381518 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "367d9a4f-b90f-54d2-9396-3fe552b009d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381519 #edb/ref 1080 "$x$ does not divide $5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381519 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381519 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8b599141-3ad2-5611-a982-461c88d2dfdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381520 #edb/ref 1080 "$5$ does not divide $x$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381520 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381520 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "32622f93-fb45-549c-959c-abe73c9d7de5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381521 #edb/ref 1080 "not $5$ divides $x$" #edb/ref 1